            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Chairman of the Federal Reserve</strong><br>\nIn 1987, President Ronald Reagan appointed Alan Greenspan as the chairman of the Federal Reserve, a role he would serve for nearly two decades across four different presidencies. As chair, he was responsible for guiding monetary policy to achieve stable economic growth, low unemployment, and low inflation.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Greenspan Era</strong><br>\nGreenspan's tenure is often called the \"Greenspan Era.\" He presided over the longest peacetime economic expansion in American history. His policies of adjusting interest rates to balance growth with inflation control were widely credited with contributing to this period of prosperity.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Economic Policies and Philosophy</strong><br>\nGreenspan was known for his belief in laissez-faire economics and was a strong advocate for free markets. He believed that markets could self-regulate and supported deregulation in various sectors of the economy. His philosophy was influenced by his association with the novelist and philosopher Ayn Rand during his younger years.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
